<p>This handler exposes a simple login endpoint that accepts an email and password, verifies the credentials, and returns a JWT token on success.</p>
<h2 id="implement-refresh-tokens" class="anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter"><span>Implement refresh tokens</span><a class="rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden" href="#implement-refresh-tokens" aria-label="Link to Implement refresh tokens"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 14 14" class="inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5"><path stroke="currentColor" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.4" d="m3.938 12.688 1.75-11.375M8.313 12.688l1.75-11.375M3.063 4.813h8.75M2.188 9.188h8.75"></path></svg></a></h2>
<p>Short-lived access tokens are more secure, but they require users to log in frequently.</p>
<p>To improve user experience while maintaining security, we can implement a refresh token system. This essentially creates a two-tier authentication system, where a long-lived refresh token is used to obtain short-lived access tokens.</p>
<p>The refresh token can be revoked if needed allowing for better control over user sessions.</p>
